Testing httpResource:


- Testing an httpResource() is very similar to testing a service that uses HttpClient. 🔥You provide
    the testing backend with provideHttpClientTesting(), inject HttpTestingController, expect the
    request, and flush the response.

- 🔥🔥If the resource is created by a service method, the call must happen in an injection context.
    You can use TestBed.runInInjectionContext() for that. Then call TestBed.tick() to let the resource
    start its request, flush the response, and wait for the application to be stable before asserting
    the resource value:

        const userService = TestBed.inject(UserService);
        const http = TestBed.inject(HttpTestingController);
        const usersResource = TestBed.runInInjectionContext(() => userService.list()); 👈
        TestBed.tick(); 👈
        http.expectOne('/users').flush([{ id: 1, name: 'Cédric' }]); 👈
        await TestBed.inject(ApplicationRef).whenStable();
        expect(usersResource.value()).toEqual([{ id: 1, name: 'Cédric' }]);
        http.verify();


- 🔥🔥When testing a component that consumes a service returning a ResourceRef, you usually don’t
    need to involve HTTP at all. Instead, mock the service method and return a fake resource. One
    convenient way to create one is to use rxResource():

        class UserListTester {
            readonly fixture = TestBed.createComponent(UserList);
            readonly users = page.getByRole('listitem');
            readonly loading = page.getByRole('status');
        }
        describe('TestingHttpResourceComponent', () => {
            let userService: Pick<Mocked<UserService>, 'list'>;
            beforeEach(() => {
                userService = { list: vi.fn().mockName('UserService.list') };
                TestBed.configureTestingModule({
                    providers: [{ provide: UserService, useValue: userService }]
                });
            });
            it('should mock a resource returned by a service', async () => {
                const users$ = new Subject<Array<UserModel>>();
                userService.list.mockImplementation(() =>
                    rxResource({
                    stream: () => users$
                    })
                );
                const tester = new UserListTester();
                await expect.element(tester.loading).toBeVisible();
                users$.next([{ id: 1, name: 'Cédric' }]);
                users$.complete();
                await expect.element(tester.users).toHaveTextContent('Cédric');
            });
        });